; File-chooser portal backend: the terminal instead of the GTK picker.
; `cmd` resolves against this directory first, then the installed share dir
; (/usr/share/xdg-desktop-portal-termfilechooser/), so portal-wrapper.sh
; beside this file wins over the stock yazi-wrapper.sh still installed there.
; Putting `yazi-wrapper.sh` back here is the whole of the way back to stock.
; The terminal choice comes in via TERMCMD below; the app-id matches the float
; rule in niri's config.kdl.
;
; Opening runs yazi, which is good at it. Saving runs portal-save.sh — fzf
; over every directory under $HOME, then a readline field for the name —
; because saving is not a browsing problem and yazi's Return has to mean
; "descend" and "commit" at once, which it cannot.
;
; save_mode=last starts each save in the directory the last one landed in,
; rather than wherever the application proposes — which for most GTK apps is
; ~/Documents regardless of what you are doing. portal-save.sh writes that
; state file itself; the portal cannot, for a file that does not exist yet.
; create_help_file=0 is load-bearing, not tidiness. Left at its default of 1
; the portal creates the suggested file before the dialog opens, writes its
; own instructions into it, and afterwards REQUIRES the path that comes back
; to already exist — filechooser.c stats it and `goto cleanup` on ENOENT,
; skipping the whole success reply, so the application is never told where to
; write and saves silently do nothing. That placeholder exists for browsers
; that can only select an entry that is already there; portal-save.sh asks for
; a name instead, so it is pure harm here. It is also what littered
; ~/Pictures and ~/Documents with 461-byte untitled.* stubs, one per dialog.
[filechooser]
cmd=portal-wrapper.sh
create_help_file=0
default_dir=$HOME
env=TERMCMD=foot --app-id=termfilechooser --title=files
open_mode=suggested
save_mode=last
